> monotone x^3
monotonic x^3:
  strictly increasing on all real x
  proved symbolically: x^3' = 3x^2; its real roots: 0 (multiplicity 2) (exact); f' keeps its sign between roots of odd multiplicity (zeros of f' of even multiplicity are isolated, so it stays strict)
> roots x^2 - 1
zeros of x^2 - 1:
  2 zeros:
    x = -1
    x = 1
  proved: a polynomial of degree 2 has 2 roots counted with multiplicity
> convex x^2
convexity of x^2:
  convex on all real x
  proved symbolically: f'' = 2, a polynomial; its sign changes only at real roots of odd multiplicity
> concave -x^2
convexity of -x^2:
  concave on all real x
  proved symbolically: f'' = -2, a polynomial; its sign changes only at real roots of odd multiplicity
> maclaurin sin(x)
Maclaurin (Taylor at 0) series of sin(x) at x = 0, through order 5:
  sin(x) = x - x^3/6 + x^5/120 + O(x^6)
  as x -> 0; coefficients c_k = f^(k)(0)/k!: 0 1 0 -1/6 0 1/120
  check: the coefficients agree with Taylor-jet arithmetic (long double) to 0
  check: |f - P| = 1.98e-11 at x = 0 ± 1/10, 1.55e-13 at ± 1/20, 1.21e-15 at ± 1/40 (shrinks like h^7.0; expected >= 6)
> gradient x^2 + y^2
∇ x^2 + y^2 = (2x, 2y)
  ∂/∂x x^2 + y^2 = 2x
  ∂/∂y x^2 + y^2 = 2y
  holds wherever the formulas are defined
  check: every entry matches central differences at 200 points (max relative difference 4e-13)
> totient 12
phi(12) = 4
  how: 12 = 2^2 * 3; phi = product of p^(e-1) (p - 1): 2^1*1 * 2
  the order of the unit group (Z/12)*; lambda(12) = 2 is its exponent
  check: counted the k in 1..12 with gcd(k, 12) = 1: 4
> multorder 3 mod 11
ord(3) = 5 mod 11
  how: the order divides lambda(11) = 10; 3^5 = 1 and 3^(5/5) = 3
  not a primitive root: phi(11) = 10
  additive order: 11
  check: stepping through the powers, 3^k = 1 first at k = 5
> invmod 3 mod 11
3^-1 = 4 mod 11
  how: extended Euclid: 4*3 - 1*11 = 1
  check: 3 * 4 = 12 = 1*11 + 1
> modinv 3 mod 11
3^-1 = 4 mod 11
  how: extended Euclid: 4*3 - 1*11 = 1
  check: 3 * 4 = 12 = 1*11 + 1
> rect (1+2i)/(3-i)
(1+2i)/(3-i) = 1/10 + 7i/10
  Re = 1/10,  Im = 7/10
  check: equal numerically (0.1 + 0.7i, complex arithmetic)
